  "summary": "Five preprints address specific error sources and resource limits in turbulence transmission, single-pixel imaging, ptychography and hyperspectral microscopy.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is recovering optical field information from constrained or imperfect measurements through computation or engineered illumination and readout. Active convolved illumination is reviewed as a means of preserving mode structure through turbulent media using segmented reciprocal-space control. Two single-pixel imaging preprints treat measurement imperfections differently: a Fourier-domain response-matrix correction addresses quantization errors in binary-encoded Fourier acquisition, while a calibration and composite-readout framework converts temporal mixing into a measurement resource. A stochastic gradient descent framework for X-ray ptychography reduces peak GPU memory to 65% and time to 22% versus the comparison method. Event-camera Fourier-transform hyperspectral microscopy with continuous birefringent interferometer scanning reaches real-time widefield spectral acquisition. Modalities differ; the novelty lies in targeted treatment of specific error sources or resource bottlenecks within each.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
